actors: Browser | Server | sessions table

step: You send a username and password
msg: Browser -> Server: POST /login
text: The only time the password travels. Everything after this step rides on
  the cookie instead.

step: The server checks the password against its hash
note: Server: verify_password()
text: The server never compares the password you typed with a stored copy. It
  hashes your attempt and compares the two hashes. This is the function you
  fix in task 1.

step: The server writes a session row
msg: Server -> sessions table: INSERT id, user, expiry
text: The row is the real session. It says which user the id belongs to and
  when it stops being valid. The id comes from `resolve_login_session_id`,
  the function you fix in task 2.

step: The browser gets a cookie holding only the id
msg: Server -> Browser: Set-Cookie: session_id
text: The cookie is `HttpOnly`, so JavaScript on the page can't read it. It
  holds an opaque id and nothing else. No name, no role, no expiry you could
  trust.

step: The browser sends it back by itself
msg: Browser -> Server: GET /sessions/mine + cookie
text: From now on the browser attaches the cookie to every request to this
  site. You don't write any code for that. It is also why the cookie is
  worth stealing: whoever presents it is treated as you.

step: The server trusts the row, not the cookie
msg: Server -> sessions table: SELECT by id
text: A cookie that matches no row, or a row that has expired, gets a 401. The
  session inspector in the app shows both sides of this step at once: an
  empty `document.cookie` next to what the server holds.
